      "id": "newton-1711",
      "why": "States the degree of exactness for Newton–Cotes (equally spaced) rules: a rule through n equally spaced nodes is exact to degree n-1 (n even) or n (n odd), illustrating that fixed equally spaced nodes are not optimal."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Using Lagrange interpolation to obtain quadrature weights from chosen nodes [lagrange-1795] and the orthogonal polynomials whose zeros lie inside [-1,1] as the natural choice of nodes for an optimal interpolatory rule [legendre-1785], one can attain exactness up to degree 2n−1 with n appropriately chosen nodes, so the fewest nodes needed for exactness to degree 7 is n=4. By contrast, Newton’s equally spaced (Newton–Cotes) rules require seven nodes to reach degree 7 when nodes are fixed and equally spaced [newton-1711]."
